### Ladders §5B hypotheses to TEST (K1459 provisional)
- events replaces chronology, L5 after standards before progressions; EVT- {kind, when EDTF+basis, where?, participants[{entity, role}], concerns[entity], sources[extent], grade, asserted_by}; doc's own date = event of the document (TEST: conflates document metadata with world event — keep doc dates as "dated facts" vs events? decide).
- kinds closed list (meeting vote adoption signing award payment transfer filing publication statement communication inspection appointment departure order hearing other)
- relations part_of authorises answers amends reverses follows_from causes(cited statement or member hypothesis); sequence computed not stored; apart from lines and law relations.
- "not found at level n"; who knew what via communication recipients.
- ~50 reqs L1-L3.
- Ladder stages: L1 events held stage2; L2 sequence/process stage 2; L3 relations stage 2; L4 reconstruction stage 3; L5 stages 3-4.
- §2 claims: "one event store, a document's own date, a vote, a payment and a statement same kind of object" (TEST).
- MONEY §5C: payments/transfers as events AND MNY- money facts with authority [event,...]. One home per fact risk: decide event owns occurrence (who, when, what act), money fact owns amount/stage/from/to and references EVT-.
- PEOPLE §5A: statements are events dated acts naming person.
- §10 cites "DEC-77 (10)" for cause: wrong, K1461 says DEC-84 (10).
- Ladder TIME §4.4 L3 says events in L5 after standards before progressions (consistent).
Problems spotted: (1) 'part_of' collides with lines kind part_of (same name, different family) — rename event relation 'within' or 'step_of'. (2) 'follows_from' ambiguous with causation; (3) 'authorises' -> conformance/powers overlap (B6 powers never verdict) — authorisation relation must be cited statement only, never inferred; (4) progressions vs events-as-process overlap: process = progression instance; event part-of process = event placed on a progression stage (one home: progressions holds declared flow & instance; events hold occurrences; placement replaces/extends document placement).
